  <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
  
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="card ci">
      <div class="callout suc">THE CONCEPTUAL HEART OF CONSOLIDATION: §297(3)'s "single enterprise" fiction is the philosophical foundation every consolidation mechanic in the following posters (capital consolidation, debt elimination, intragroup profit elimination) exists to implement. It also happens to be one of the strongest points of genuine conceptual convergence with IFRS 10 anywhere in this entire poster series.</div>

  
      <div class="ctitle ci">The philosophical foundation beneath every consolidation mechanic to come</div>
      <div class="principle">Having established WHO must consolidate (§290) and WHICH subsidiaries are included (§§294–296), this Titel answers WHAT the resulting Konzernabschluss must actually look like, and WHY its mechanics take the specific shape they do. §297(3)'s "single enterprise" fiction is the conceptual key: every later consolidation technique — capital consolidation, debt elimination, intragroup profit elimination — exists SOLELY to make the group's combined accounts look as though the parent and its subsidiaries were genuinely one economic entity, DESPITE remaining separate legal persons.</div>
      <div class="elabel">HOW §§297–299 MAP TO GERMAN ACCOUNTING PRINCIPLES</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Wirtschaftliche Betrachtungsweise, at its most complete expression</strong> — the ENTIRE Konzernabschluss is built on looking past legal entity boundaries to economic reality</span>
        <span><strong>Klarer Einblick, once again</strong> — §297's own true-and-fair language directly parallels §243(2) and §264(2), now applied to the group as a whole</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">What §§297–299 require, at a glance</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-06-17"><span>§</span><span>Core requirement</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>297</span><span>Components required; the single-enterprise fiction; the group-level true-and-fair remedy</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>298</span><span>Individual-accounts rules apply correspondingly; limited format flexibility</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>299</span><span>Reference date rules, including the Zwischenabschluss requirement for misaligned year-ends</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Every parent preparing a Konzernabschluss under §290</div>
      <div class="principle">§§297–299 govern the content, form, and reference date of every Konzernabschluss a parent is required (or chooses) to prepare under §290, having survived the §§291–293 and §§294–296 scope tests established in the two preceding posters.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>§297(1) — the Components of the Konzernabschluss</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Three mandatory components, with two more for capital-market-oriented parents</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th"><span>Component</span><span>Who must prepare it</span></div>
        <div class="tr"><span>Konzernbilanz (consolidated balance sheet)</span><span>Every parent preparing a Konzernabschluss</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt"><span>Konzern-Gewinn- und Verlustrechnung</span><span>Every parent preparing a Konzernabschluss</span></div>
        <div class="tr"><span>Konzernanhang (consolidated notes)</span><span>Every parent preparing a Konzernabschluss</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt"><span>Konzern-Kapitalflussrechnung, Konzern-Eigenkapitalspiegel</span><span>Capital-market-oriented parents specifically — directly paralleling §264(1a)'s individual-entity equivalent established earlier in this series</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Segment reporting may additionally be included, though its precise mandatory/optional status for different parent categories is worth confirming against current text if it matters for a specific filing.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>§297(3) — the "Single Enterprise" Fiction</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">As if the entire group were one single enterprise</div>
      <div class="principle">§297(3) requires the Konzernabschluss to present the net assets, financial position, and results of operations of the group AS IF the included companies formed a SINGLE ENTERPRISE ("als ob es sich bei den einbezogenen Unternehmen um ein einziges Unternehmen handelte") — this single sentence is arguably the most conceptually important provision in this entire Titel. Every subsequent consolidation mechanic — capital consolidation (eliminating the parent's investment against the subsidiary's equity), debt consolidation (eliminating intragroup receivables/payables), and intragroup profit elimination — exists purely to give effect to this ONE underlying fiction.</div>
      <div class="callout suc">This is a genuinely strong point of conceptual convergence with IFRS: IFRS 10's own consolidation objective is built on essentially the SAME "single economic entity" concept — both frameworks look past the LEGAL reality of separate corporate persons to present the GROUP's combined economic substance as a unified whole.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Worked Example — Seeing the Single-Entity Fiction in Action</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Intragroup sales simply disappear, exactly as they would within one company</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 1: An intragroup sale that never happened, from the group's perspective</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A parent manufactures a component and sells it to its own subsidiary for €500,000, who then incorporates it into a finished product sold to an external customer for €900,000. Each entity's OWN individual accounts show its own respective revenue.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">In the Konzernabschluss, the €500,000 intragroup sale is ELIMINATED entirely — from the GROUP's perspective, as a single enterprise, the component was simply MOVED internally from one department to another, generating NO external revenue at that stage. Only the FINAL €900,000 sale to the genuinely external customer appears as group revenue — exactly how a single, undivided company's own internal transfer between departments would never appear as revenue on its own individual accounts either.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>The Group-Level True-and-Fair Remedy</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The same disclosure-only mechanism, now at group level</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§297 requires that where special circumstances mean the Konzernabschluss does NOT convey a picture corresponding to the actual circumstances of the group's net assets, financial position, and results of operations, ADDITIONAL disclosures must be made in the Konzernanhang — directly PARALLELING §264(2)'s individual-entity true-and-fair remedy established earlier in this series, now simply operating at the GROUP level rather than the single-entity level. The remedy remains DISCLOSURE, never departure from a specific consolidation rule.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>§298(1) — Borrowing the Entire Individual-Accounts Framework by Reference</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">The Konzernabschluss does not reinvent measurement rules from scratch</div>
      <div class="principle">§298(1) provides that the general provisions governing INDIVIDUAL Kapitalgesellschaft accounts — everything this poster series has covered on recognition (§§246–251), measurement (§§252–256a), the Bilanz and GuV formats (§§266–278), and much else besides — apply CORRESPONDINGLY (entsprechend) to the Konzernabschluss, UNLESS the specific consolidation provisions provide otherwise. This is a genuinely important STRUCTURAL choice: rather than legislating an entirely separate measurement and recognition framework for group accounts, HGB simply BORROWS the individual-accounts framework wholesale, and the consolidation-specific Titel only ADD what is genuinely different about group reporting (elimination entries, uniform group-wide measurement, goodwill arising on capital consolidation).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Testing a Measurement Election at Group Level</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">The same LIFO election, now applying group-wide</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Applying the §256 LIFO election consistently across the group</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A parent elects LIFO (per §256, established in the earlier §§252–256a poster) for a specific inventory category in its own individual accounts. Its subsidiary holds the SAME category of inventory.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Because §298(1) applies §256's LIFO rule CORRESPONDINGLY to the Konzernabschluss, and §252(1) no.6's Bewertungsstetigkeit (consistency) principle carries through as well, the parent must apply a UNIFORM measurement method across the ENTIRE group for consolidation purposes — if the subsidiary's own individual accounts used FIFO instead, its figures must be RESTATED to LIFO (or the group must otherwise reconcile the difference) before consolidation, since the group accounts cannot present the same economic item measured two different ways depending on which legal entity happens to hold it.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>§298(2) — Format Flexibility for Group-Specific Circumstances</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A group-level parallel to §265(4)'s individual-entity flexibility</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§298(2) permits certain items in the Konzernbilanz and Konzern-GuV to be COMBINED or ADAPTED differently than the individual-entity format would otherwise require, WHERE the group's own specific circumstances justify it — WITH the deviation disclosed in the Konzernanhang — directly paralleling the §265(4) individual-entity adaptation flexibility established in the earlier Bilanz poster, now exercised at group level to accommodate genuinely group-specific presentation needs.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>§299(1) — the Ordinary Reference Date Rule</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The parent's own balance sheet date, as the group's reference point</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§299(1) establishes that the Konzernabschluss is generally prepared as of the SAME balance sheet date as the parent's own individual annual accounts — the parent's own fiscal year-end becomes the GROUP's reference date for consolidation purposes.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>§299(2) — the Zwischenabschluss Requirement for a &gt;3-Month Gap</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A special interim statement, purely for consolidation purposes</div>
      <div class="principle">§299(2) requires that WHERE a subsidiary's own balance sheet date differs from the parent's by MORE than three months, that subsidiary must prepare a special interim financial statement (Zwischenabschluss) as of the PARENT's own reference date, SOLELY for consolidation purposes — ensuring genuine comparability across group members, REGARDLESS of each individual entity's own statutory year-end.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Worked Example — Preparing a Zwischenabschluss</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A genuine gap, requiring genuine extra work</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 3: A subsidiary with a materially different fiscal year-end</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A German parent's fiscal year ends 31 December. A recently-acquired foreign subsidiary has its own statutory fiscal year ending 30 June — a six-month gap, well beyond the three-month threshold.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The subsidiary must prepare a Zwischenabschluss as of 31 December (the PARENT's own reference date) SPECIFICALLY for consolidation purposes — this is IN ADDITION to, not instead of, its own separate 30 June statutory accounts required under its own local law, representing genuine additional preparation effort purely to satisfy the group's consolidation timing needs.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>§299(3) — the ≤3-Month Gap Exception</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A practical relief, provided material events in the gap are still captured</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§299(3) permits, WHERE the gap is THREE MONTHS OR LESS, the subsidiary's OWN existing accounts to be used DIRECTLY for consolidation, WITHOUT preparing a separate Zwischenabschluss — PROVIDED any material events occurring in the gap period between the subsidiary's own year-end and the parent's reference date are appropriately taken into account or disclosed, so genuinely significant developments are never simply missed due to the timing mismatch.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Worked Example — Using the Subsidiary's Own Accounts With a Material-Event Adjustment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A narrower gap, genuine relief, but still no blind spots permitted</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 4: A subsidiary with a two-month gap and a significant intervening event</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A German parent's fiscal year ends 31 December. A subsidiary's own fiscal year ends 31 October — a two-month gap, within the §299(3) threshold. Between 31 October and 31 December, the subsidiary suffers a significant, uninsured warehouse fire loss.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The parent may use the subsidiary's OWN 31 October accounts directly for consolidation, WITHOUT preparing a separate Zwischenabschluss — but the SIGNIFICANT warehouse fire loss occurring in the two-month gap period must still be appropriately taken into account or disclosed within the Konzernabschluss, ensuring this genuinely material event isn't simply lost in the timing mismatch between the two reference dates.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 15 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Whether a specific event occurring within a ≤3-month gap period is genuinely "material" enough to require adjustment or disclosure under §299(3)</span>
            <span>Whether a proposed §298(2) format adaptation is genuinely justified by group-specific circumstances, versus merely a preference</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Harmonising measurement methods across a large, multi-jurisdictional group under §298(1)'s "corresponding application" requirement, particularly for newly-acquired subsidiaries</span>
            <span>The genuine administrative burden of preparing a Zwischenabschluss for subsidiaries with a &gt;3-month year-end gap, especially where local statutory accounts alone would otherwise have sufficed</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 16 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS (IFRS 10, IFRS 3)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — HGB §§297–299 vs IFRS</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>HGB</span><span>IFRS</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Underlying consolidation philosophy</span><span>Single enterprise fiction (§297(3))</span><span>Single economic entity concept (IFRS 10) — CONVERGED</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Uniform group-wide measurement</span><span>Required via §298(1)'s "corresponding application"</span><span>Required — IFRS 10 mandates uniform accounting policies across the group — CONVERGED</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Reference date gap tolerance</span><span>Explicit 3-month bright-line threshold (§299)</span><span>IFRS 10 permits a maximum 3-month gap as well, with adjustment for significant transactions — CONVERGED</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">This Titel is, alongside §272(1)'s treasury share treatment, one of the STRONGEST points of genuine convergence anywhere in this poster series — the core consolidation philosophy, the uniform-measurement requirement, and even the specific 3-month reference-date threshold all align closely between HGB and IFRS.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 17 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Where every §§297–299 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / obligation</span><span>Document(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Intragroup transaction eliminated</span><span><span class="badge bo">Konzernbilanz</span> <span class="badge bp">Konzern-GuV</span></span><span>Gives effect to the §297(3) single-enterprise fiction</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Group-level true-and-fair shortfall</span><span><span class="badge be">Konzernanhang</span></span><span>Additional disclosure, not departure from consolidation rules</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Zwischenabschluss prepared</span><span>—</span><span>A standalone document feeding INTO consolidation, not itself a filed statement</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 18 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>Related Provisions — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How §§297–299 connect across HGB Book 3</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Provision</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§264(1a), §264(2)</span><span>The individual-entity equivalents §297(1)'s extra components and §297's true-and-fair remedy directly parallel</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§§252–256a</span><span>The measurement rules §298(1) applies "correspondingly" to the Konzernabschluss</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§265(4)</span><span>The individual-entity format flexibility §298(2) mirrors at group level</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§§300–307</span><span>The NEXT Titel, supplying the actual mechanical techniques (capital consolidation, debt elimination) that implement the §297(3) single-enterprise fiction</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 19 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s19"><span class="num">19</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="three-col">
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Einheitstheorie / Einziges Unternehmen</div><div class="glossary-def">The "single enterprise" fiction underlying all consolidation mechanics — the group is presented as though it were one company.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Konzernbilanz / Konzern-GuV / Konzernanhang</div><div class="glossary-def">The three mandatory components of every Konzernabschluss.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Entsprechend anzuwenden</div><div class="glossary-def">"Applied correspondingly" — the mechanism by which individual-accounts rules are borrowed wholesale for consolidated accounts under §298(1).</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Zwischenabschluss</div><div class="glossary-def">An interim financial statement a subsidiary must prepare, purely for consolidation purposes, when its own year-end differs from the parent's by more than three months.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Stichtag</div><div class="glossary-def">Reference/balance sheet date — generally the parent's own, per §299(1).</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Konzern-Eigenkapitalspiegel</div><div class="glossary-def">Consolidated statement of changes in equity, mandatory for capital-market-oriented parents under §297(1).</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="legend">
    <div class="ltitle">Key</div>
    <div class="lrow">
      <span class="badge bo">Konzernbilanz</span> Consolidated balance sheet
      <span class="badge bp">Konzern-GuV</span> Consolidated income statement
      <span class="badge be">Konzernanhang</span> Consolidated notes
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
